import{_ as t,o as a,c as i,ak as n}from"./chunks/framework.B8jO0odJ.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Chainy current project baseline","description":"Capstone project title: Chainy: A 3D World Educational Game for learning emerging Technology (Blockchain & AI)","frontmatter":{"title":"Chainy current project baseline","description":"Capstone project title: Chainy: A 3D World Educational Game for learning emerging Technology (Blockchain & AI)","source":"docs/project-baseline.md","category":"Start here","sourceUpdated":"2026-10-08T07:30:14+08:00","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"content/docs/project-baseline.md","filePath":"content/docs/project-baseline.md"}'),r={name:"content/docs/project-baseline.md"};function o(s,e,d,c,l,p){return a(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<div><h1 id="chainy-current-project-baseline">Chainy current project baseline<a class="header-anchor" href="#chainy-current-project-baseline" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h1><p><strong>Capstone project title:</strong> Chainy: A 3D World Educational Game for learning emerging Technology (Blockchain &amp; AI)</p><h2 id="project-naming-decision--october-8-2026">Project naming decision — October 8, 2026<a class="header-anchor" href="#project-naming-decision--october-8-2026" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>The user confirmed the exact capstone title above, including capitalization. Use it on formal project covers, the manuscript and website metadata. Use <strong>Chainy</strong> as the short product name and <strong>CHAINY</strong> in the logo. The title retains “3D World”; gameplay scope continues to follow the approved tappable three-world map and rounded 3D visual direction.</p><h2 id="governing-mission-and-scope">Governing mission and scope<a class="header-anchor" href="#governing-mission-and-scope" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p><strong>Deliver an offline-ready educational game for foundational learning about Web3, AI and blockchain for learners aged 12–17, especially learners in low-income and remote communities with limited access to technology.</strong> Confirmed by the user on October 8, 2026. This mission is the source of truth for product, content, design and delivery decisions.</p><p>Teach only the essential foundations needed for this capstone. The earlier request for the whole Web3 encyclopedia A–Z is explicitly withdrawn. Use the encyclopedia as a source for selected concepts and prepare beginner AI materials; source wording must be checked for accuracy. Do not assume prior knowledge of computers, cryptocurrency or AI, ownership of a phone, or access to the internet at home. Support a short touch-control introduction, plain English, readable visual examples, self-paced retries and distinct profiles on shared devices. Language and reading accessibility must be checked with the actual cohort; English first remains the current decision.</p><p>The user approves a tappable map with three worlds: Web3, Blockchain and AI, leading directly to short guided lessons. Preserve the approved toy-like 3D visual identity; walkable 3D exploration is no longer required. Defer free spatial construction and use bounded record ordering/linking. Keep guided comparison of three local replicas and a short fixed-grid observe-decide-act challenge. Validate the genuine small example-trained classifier before committing to its implementation. No cloud service is required for these lessons.</p><p>The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/learning-foundations.html">beginner learning materials</a> define a proposed nine-lesson content package, with examples, activities, questions and answer explanations. Nine lessons is a drafting default, not a user-mandated count or tested session duration. Scope additions must serve the mission, have an observable learning outcome and fit the protected integration, QA, manuscript and rehearsal schedule before <strong>November 21, 2026</strong>.</p><h2 id="documentation-publication-decision--october-8-2026">Documentation publication decision — October 8, 2026<a class="header-anchor" href="#documentation-publication-decision--october-8-2026" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>The user approved a public Markdown documentation viewer containing all project Markdown, including archived material and repository/agent guidance. Canonical source remains private in Chainy. The organization permits public Pages but rejects repository deploy keys needed by the publisher. The user&#39;s authorized personal-account fallback, public owenlim225/Chainy-Docs, therefore hosts the generated website using a repository-scoped deploy key. Automatic updates follow committed source changes. See <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/tools/docs-viewer/readme.html">viewer operations</a>. This is management tooling, not learner functionality or evidence of game completion. Preserve the November 21, 2026 pre-final and integration, QA, pilot and rehearsal time.</p><p>Updated October 8, 2026 from the user&#39;s latest decisions. This is the development planning baseline, not proof of adviser approval. Historical proposals and classroom reports remain source evidence and may contradict it.</p><h2 id="confirmed-decisions">Confirmed decisions<a class="header-anchor" href="#confirmed-decisions" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><ul><li>Audience is ages 12–17, junior to senior high school. The user wants simple, engaging gamified education inspired by the supplied Giggle Academy concept paper. Earlier outreach sites remain context; the possible school beta is recorded below.</li><li>Three thematic worlds are confirmed: Web3, Blockchain and AI. The earlier two-world/three-level organization is superseded; the lesson sequence and count require revision.</li><li>Essential foundational content only; full A–Z coverage is withdrawn. The user authorizes preparation of beginner AI materials.</li><li>Approved mechanic revision: defer free construction, use bounded ordering/linking, keep guided three-replica comparison, prototype the example-trained classifier, and keep a short fixed-grid agent exercise. CH-R02 is explicitly revised below rather than silently treated as satisfied by the old acceptance test.</li><li>Navigation is a tappable three-world map leading directly to short lessons. This supersedes required walkable 3D exploration; approved character/art direction remains.</li><li>The user accepts a presentation adapted for teenagers, badges and level progress as the initial reward system, and an assistant recommendation for the AI implementation.</li><li>Local SQLite supersedes JSON as the primary save store. JSON may still be an export/interchange format.</li><li>Gameplay must work offline. The user proposes a teacher/representative who distributes devices or APKs and carries evaluation data and updates; exact operation remains a recommendation pending site details.</li><li>This repository is the management and documentation hub. Game source belongs in a separate linked repository; the authorized private repository has been created at <a href="https://github.com/Kamiyon-Studio/Chainy-Game">https://github.com/Kamiyon-Studio/Chainy-Game</a>.</li><li>User reports zero implementation progress as of October 5, 2026. Earlier extracted Done/In Progress statuses are historical and cannot establish current implementation progress.</li><li>Official project cycle remains September 2026–April 2027. Pre-final defense is November 21, 2026 and requires 60 percent completion. The supplied rubric has four categories and eight unnamed criteria worth four points each (32 total). It describes quality bands, not what constitutes 60 percent of the project. Criterion descriptions and the completion formula remain unknown; mandatory artifacts are recorded below.</li><li>User authorizes creation of mentor skills and a task/requirements board. GitHub Projects is selected to keep management alongside this repository.</li></ul><h2 id="device-and-identity-planning-baseline">Device and identity planning baseline<a class="header-anchor" href="#device-and-identity-planning-baseline" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>English first is confirmed. Team work is assigned by names; lack of GitHub accounts does not block delegation. See <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/team-delegation.html">team packets</a>.</p><p>The user explicitly retains Android 10+/11+, 4 GB physical RAM, 64 GB total device storage, a 64-bit ARM CPU, OpenGL ES 3.2 or Vulkan 1.1, and an entry-level-to-midrange GPU as the target specification. The working test interpretation remains Android 11+ provisionally with Android 10 as a compatibility candidate; it does not replace the supplied specification or establish tested support. The graphics path and measured minimum remain unverified. Total capacity is not the game&#39;s required space. See <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/hardware-validation.html">hardware validation</a>.</p><p>The user proposes teacher-issued learner codes entered on each device. Adopt this as offline profile identification and pseudonymous research linkage, not verified authentication. Code entry on another device does not restore local progress. See <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/technical-design.html">technical design</a>.</p><p>Single-player, Unity/C# and touchscreen controls remain the technical direction. Engine patch, render pipeline and SQLite adapter must be pinned during the device spike. No real public blockchain or online multiplayer is requested.</p><h2 id="recommended-bounded-implementation">Recommended bounded implementation<a class="header-anchor" href="#recommended-bounded-implementation" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>October 8 leaves 44 calendar days before defense, with an earlier October 30 candidate needed for the possible November 3–6 beta. Capacity is unknown, so there is no evidence-backed completion promise. The approved simpler navigation and selected foundations reduce scope, but the revised lessons still need an end-to-end device prototype and content-production timing before a delivery forecast can be validated.</p><p>Use reusable selection, sorting, sequencing and scenario templates with fixed activity views. The approved replacement for free spatial construction is bounded record ordering/linking with explanatory feedback. Three logical blockchain nodes run on one device; guided comparison replaces spatial network construction. Broader sandbox work is deferred beyond the pre-final and is not promised for a later release.</p><p>Recommend a tiny local nearest-centroid classifier over explicit numeric features of fictional objects, built in C#. Learners label examples, compute class prototypes, test held-out examples, inspect errors, and change the examples. Include deterministic ties and insufficient-example feedback. This is an actual small example-trained model, not a cloud LLM or computer vision model. Confirm the prototype meets the learning outcome before production. <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.neighbors.NearestCentroid.html">Algorithm reference</a>; this reference does not prescribe a Python dependency.</p><p>Recommend one short observe-decide-act agent route with visible rules and feedback. It teaches agent behavior; do not claim it is generative AI or reinforcement learning.</p><p>Recommend no microphone, cloud sync, educator web dashboard, real cryptocurrency, content downloader, currency economy, or certificates before defense. These exclusions are recommendations where not previously confirmed, not silent removal of required features.</p><h2 id="traceable-requirements">Traceable requirements<a class="header-anchor" href="#traceable-requirements" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Observable acceptance evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CH-R01</td><td>Offline Android guided learning</td><td>Fresh installed build opens the three-world map and completes a packaged lesson in airplane mode on the agreed device</td></tr><tr><td>CH-R02</td><td>Bounded record assembly; revised by user approval October 8</td><td>Player orders and links supplied records, tests an invalid sequence, receives an explanation and corrects it; free spatial placement and multiple layouts are no longer acceptance requirements</td></tr><tr><td>CH-R03</td><td>Linked records and ordering</td><td>The Blockchain lesson checks a documented ordering rule and displays linked records with correct feedback</td></tr><tr><td>CH-R04</td><td>Validation and tamper detection</td><td>Altering a record is detected against the documented reference; valid correction succeeds</td></tr><tr><td>CH-R05</td><td>Multi-node simulation</td><td>Three local replicas demonstrate agreement, disagreement and recovery under an explicitly simplified rule</td></tr><tr><td>CH-R06</td><td>Example-based classification</td><td>Changing training examples can change predictions on held-out items; errors and limitations are explained</td></tr><tr><td>CH-R07</td><td>Agent activity</td><td>Learner changes a visible action policy and observes a route succeed or fail with feedback</td></tr><tr><td>CH-R08</td><td>Assessments and progression</td><td>Essential lessons in all three worlds map objectives to activities and checks; completion and badges persist</td></tr><tr><td>CH-R09</td><td>SQLite persistence</td><td>Distinct local profiles and sessions survive relaunch; chosen Android binding and schema migration pass device tests</td></tr><tr><td>CH-R10</td><td>Teacher evidence export</td><td>Consistent pseudonymous records export offline; repeat imports do not duplicate attempts</td></tr><tr><td>CH-R11</td><td>Update and recovery</td><td>A signed update preserves tested records; failed export is retryable without deletion</td></tr><tr><td>CH-R12</td><td>Defense evidence</td><td>Rubric-to-artifact mapping, reproducible APK, known limitations and rehearsed demonstration</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="source-precedence-and-open-decisions">Source precedence and open decisions<a class="header-anchor" href="#source-precedence-and-open-decisions" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Direct user decisions supersede this document; update it when new decisions arrive. The approved manuscript/rubric is still needed to distinguish product intent from school assessment requirements. Storyboard lesson counters are illustrative, not extra approved levels.</p><p>Needed: actual criterion descriptions; sustainable per-person availability; test devices; pilot cohort/site arrangements; evaluation questions and protocol; teacher custodian and approved data destination. See <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/offline-data-plan.html">offline collection</a>, <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/kanban-plan-draft.html">active schedule</a>, and <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/mentors.html">mentors</a>.</p><h2 id="team-and-evidence-update">Team and evidence update<a class="header-anchor" href="#team-and-evidence-update" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Confirmed roles: Sherwin Limosnero (project lead); Christian Jude Villaber (technical lead); Sean Matthew Narvaez (documentation lead); Luis Cabrido III (art lead); Miguel De Leon (UI and documentation support). Intermediate experience is self-reported. Availability of 5–8 hours daily is provisional and is not established for each member. AI assistance is a tool, not verified team capacity or evidence of correctness.</p><p>The latest user proposal uses teacher-issued learner codes entered on devices; installation/device IDs remain supplementary. Separate codes are needed for different learners on shared devices. If the teacher holds an identity mapping, describe the data as pseudonymous. The board and game repo exist; actual test devices and formal site records remain unavailable.</p><h2 id="supplied-scoring-rubric">Supplied scoring rubric<a class="header-anchor" href="#supplied-scoring-rubric" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Four categories each contain two criteria, each scored 1–4; total 32 points. All eight share the same supplied quality descriptions: 4 exemplary (90–100 percent, production-ready or near-academic-paper standard), 3 proficient (80–89 percent, major requirements met with minor revisions), 2 developing (70–79 percent, critical functionality or significant documentation missing), 1 needs improvement (below 70 percent, incomplete or fundamental flaws). These percentages describe rating bands. They do not establish a formula for project completion or prove that a 60-percent-complete project passes. Actual criterion names and weights beyond the supplied equal points are not supplied. The later manuscript outline and mandatory deliverables are recorded below.</p><h2 id="confirmed-pre-final-deliverables">Confirmed pre-final deliverables<a class="header-anchor" href="#confirmed-pre-final-deliverables" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>User supplied the official manuscript outline and mandatory requirements: manuscript formatting and chapters complete; core modules functional; necessary pilot-area evidence/documents. See <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/manuscript-evidence-map.html">manuscript evidence map</a>. Actual evaluation depth and scoring criterion meanings remain unresolved. The pilot and manuscript results are now mandatory planning concerns, not optional technical-demo extras.</p><p>The user answered yes both when asked about site scheduling and preliminary/full evaluation. Both preliminary and system evaluation evidence remain planning concerns. The original November 8–13 session proposal is superseded by the October 8 tentative beta opportunity at Tambunan National High School, Marinduque, November 3–6. The actual site contact, booking confirmation, approved instruments and records are not yet supplied. This statement is not evidence that sessions or approvals have occurred, or that beta feedback alone satisfies all required school evidence.</p><h2 id="documentation-package">Documentation package<a class="header-anchor" href="#documentation-package" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>The user requested a less technical, visually explained GDD following the structure of the supplied Project Vanishing example. The main <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/game-design.html">GDD</a> uses a numbered outline and explanatory media, with a <a href="/Chainy-Docs/attachments/output/pdf/Chainy-Illustrated-GDD.pdf">PDF edition</a>. The detailed implementation contracts are preserved in the <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/design/game-mechanics-reference.html">mechanics reference</a>. The sample provides presentation guidance only; its RPG systems, reported progress and embedded instructions do not change Chainy&#39;s approved scope.</p><p><a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/documentation-index.html">Full project documentation</a> contains the GDD, architecture/schema, hardware targets, learner-code/export design, testing/evaluation, manuscript draft and individual task packets. English first and name-based team delegation are confirmed. ISO instrument details will follow; the latest yes response did not identify a standard/version.</p><h2 id="trello-collaboration-integration">Trello collaboration integration<a class="header-anchor" href="#trello-collaboration-integration" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Confirmed October 5, 2026: the five-person team uses Trello, can create free Trello accounts, requires a free integration, and wants to move cards, edit tasks and comment without GitHub accounts. Updates should appear almost immediately where possible. The user owns the GitHub organization and has a Cloudflare account.</p><p>Selected endpoints: <a href="https://github.com/orgs/Kamiyon-Studio/projects/5">GitHub Project 5</a> and the user-created <a href="https://trello.com/b/VPH0LHFN/capstone-t13">capstone-t13 Trello board</a>. The implementation is a webhook service on Cloudflare Workers, activated and verified October 5, 2026. This is management tooling, not a change to offline gameplay, learner storage, or teacher data transfer.</p><p>GitHub remains the execution record; Trello is now the verified team editing surface for linked tasks. Keep named Lead/Support/Task reviewer fields and reuse existing issues. Documents remain requirements/decisions and backlog.json remains the publication inventory. Do not infer completion from card movements: Done still requires linked acceptance evidence and review.</p><p>Measured evidence (October 5, 2026): 26 GitHub items match 26 unique Trello cards with Todo/In Progress/Done lists. CAPSTONE T13 is Workspace-visible. The user explicitly approved Cloudflare credential storage and the upload completed. Both webhooks and <a href="https://chainy-kanban-sync.hainy.workers.dev">https://chainy-kanban-sync.hainy.workers.dev</a> are active. Ten local tests/type checks passed, followed by live title/status/description/new-comment checks in both directions. Sample field-update observations were 6.8 seconds Trello to GitHub and 2.8 seconds GitHub to Trello. Unsigned requests were rejected and Project webhook replay was accepted without duplicate work. Final status showed 26 links, zero pending jobs and zero failures. Temporary changes were restored and test comments removed. Token renewal is required by November 4, 2026. See ../tools/kanban-sync/README.md for operations; backlog.json records each card URL.</p><p>Scope-mentor verdict: proceed with the validated bounded integration; defer expansion. This removes an account barrier but contributes no direct learner functionality. Prerequisites are Trello authorization, a durable mapping/retry mechanism, scoped GitHub authorization and a free-tier resource check. A rough planning allowance is one focused day for a constrained prototype and one additional day for failure/recovery testing, assuming working service permissions; this is not measured capacity or a delivery promise. Stop and reassess if the prototype consumes that allowance. This work competes with the October 9–11 Android/device spike, not with reserved November integration, QA and rehearsal time before the November 21, 2026 pre-final.</p><p>Verified acceptance and limits: one task passed live field and new-comment checks before bulk import; all 26 task/card pairs were compared after import. Duplicate delivery and simulated API failure/retry passed local runtime tests; signed delivery and replay were checked live. Same-field simultaneous conflicts choose GitHub; independent fields merge. New comments retain author attribution, but edits/deletions do not mirror. Unlinked new cards, attachments and separate checklist/member/custom-field edits are outside this version. GitHub remains the execution record; teammates can update linked tasks in Trello. No paid plan was enabled; continued operation remains subject to account resource limits and credential validity.</p><p>Trello presentation update (October 5, 2026): all 26 linked cards have their existing target dates as due dates at 23:59 Asia/Manila, a colored label naming the task lead, and an Acceptance checklist with the original two acceptance items (52 total). The user explicitly chose labels only for now; actual Trello member assignments are deferred until teammates join. Original acceptance text remains in the shared description. Checklist ticks and independent edits to dates/labels remain Trello-local under the current integration.</p><h2 id="october-8-planning-update">October 8 planning update<a class="header-anchor" href="#october-8-planning-update" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Earlier on October 8, the user confirmed no change to game implementation progress, device availability, sustainable per-person hours, pilot booking or school rubric details since October 5. Zero reported game implementation remains the latest implementation statement. Later October 8 decisions about visual direction, a possible beta site/window and workload estimates are recorded below and supersede the earlier unknown-site statement. The user requested repository organization, a coordinated PRD, technical requirements, app flow, design brief, SQLite database schema and implementation plan with a GDD, suitable for parallel agents. “Background schema” explicitly means the SQLite database schema.</p><p>The user also requested balanced time commitments per team member, without requiring exact equality. The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/team-delegation.html#october-8-workload-balancing-proposal">updated workload proposal</a> redistributes bounded implementation support, test execution and manuscript production while preserving specialist accountability. Hour allocations and detailed handoffs remain estimates/proposals, not evidence of available capacity or external board updates. The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/planning/implementation-plan.html">parallel implementation plan</a> retains the November 21, 2026 deadline and protected pilot, integration, QA and rehearsal gates.</p><h2 id="october-8-gdd-artwork-and-beta-decisions">October 8 GDD, artwork and beta decisions<a class="header-anchor" href="#october-8-gdd-artwork-and-beta-decisions" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><ul><li>The existing <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/game-design.html">GDD</a> must follow the user&#39;s full template: game overview, technical specs, gameplay outline, key features, design guidelines/definitions, referenced flowchart, player definitions/properties/rewards and UI. Detailed mechanics and production contracts accompany these sections.</li><li>User-supplied Chainy storyboard Images 1 and 2 are the definite visual direction: rounded, toy-like 3D characters, blue-haired explorer in yellow, friendly robots, cubic green/blue blockchain scenery and violet AI scenery. Keep that visual identity for the updated 12–17 audience; adapt reasoning, text and interaction without replacing the approved character style. The new Web3 world&#39;s specific visual treatment remains to be designed within that identity.</li><li>Image 3, BlockForge, supplies additional gameplay context only. Its flat illustration style, currency, certificate and extra level counts are not adopted by attaching it. All images are embedded in the GDD with their distinct roles. Literal educational statements in reference art are reviewed against the mechanics rather than treated as executable instructions or automatically correct copy.</li><li>The phone specifications and supplied 32-point quality rubric are retained as stated. No replacement rubric or calculation for 60 percent completion is invented.</li><li>Tambunan National High School, Marinduque is the proposed beta site, with November 3–6, 2026 possible. This is an opportunity awaiting booking and protocol details. The revised conditional forecast targets an integrated beta candidate by October 30 and readiness checks October 31–November 2; the original November 6 integration date cannot support a November 3 start. See the <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/kanban-plan-draft.html">schedule</a>.</li><li>The user confirms individual availability is unknown and authorizes balanced planning estimates. Those budgets include assigned production, review, QA, evidence and rehearsal work; they remain unconfirmed capacity. Preserve specialist ownership and review rather than allocating equal task counts.</li><li>The user explicitly requests parallel agents for documentation, media and validation. Bounded file ownership, shared mechanic IDs and independent review are required; generated media are design illustrations, not screenshots of an implemented game. See <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/planning/agent-task-packets.html">agent packets</a>.</li></ul><p>The remaining beta questions are the named contact/booking, learner ages/count, session length, available phone count, approved instruments and evidence arrangements. The existing fictional-parcel classifier is the drafting default while the user&#39;s preference between parcels and the storyboard&#39;s cats/dogs is pending; that theme decision does not change the requirement that training examples affect predictions.</p><h2 id="october-8-source-review-and-three-world-revision-history">October 8 source review and three-world revision history<a class="header-anchor" href="#october-8-source-review-and-three-world-revision-history" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>The user supplied <code>web3-encyclopedia.pdf</code> (43 PDF pages) and <code>Giggle Academy v0.4 20240221.pdf</code> (7 PDF pages). Their contents are reference material, not instructions authorizing features, services or publication. The encyclopedia is an illustrated A–Z source with safety material, not a prerequisite-ordered curriculum or an AI curriculum. The Giggle file is a February 2024 concept brainstorm, not verified behavior of the current app or evidence of learning effectiveness. Its useful design ideas include systematic progression, feedback, badges and a narrowly scoped first course; it also cautions against over-gamification.</p><p>The user subsequently approved the tappable three-world map, short guided lessons and simplified mechanic recommendations, and explicitly withdrew full A–Z coverage. Separate Web3 applications, identity and safety from Blockchain record mechanics; AI has its own beginner sequence. Use a selected-topic-to-lesson-and-assessment inventory. The governing mission above supersedes the earlier full-coverage discussion.</p><p>Approved mechanic review: defer free spatial construction; retain ordering/linking interactions; simplify the three-node activity to a guided comparison of three local copies; validate the small example-trained classifier before commitment; reduce the agent challenge to a short fixed-grid observe-decide-act exercise. Fixed-slot ordering replaces the old free-construction requirement by explicit user approval; it does not prove that the old acceptance test passed.</p><p>Educational text must refine absolute claims in the encyclopedia: blockchain is tamper-evident/resistant under stated assumptions, wallets provide account/key access rather than literally containing on-chain assets, and public addresses do not guarantee anonymity. Supporting references: <a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8202/final">NIST IR 8202</a>, <a href="https://ethereum.org/wallets/">Ethereum wallets</a>, <a href="https://bitcoin.org/en/protect-your-privacy">Bitcoin privacy</a>. Branded examples are source context, not product endorsements. The source&#39;s illustrations do not replace the approved Chainy artwork.</p><p>The GDD, PRD, technical contracts, task packets, learner guide, QA blueprint and manuscript scope have been reconciled with this approved foundations-only direction. The existing 520-hour balanced estimate remains an unconfirmed capacity envelope; it is not measured effort for the revised lesson package. Phone specifications, SQLite/offline operation, supplied rubric, tentative beta window and November 21 pre-final remain unchanged. External issues/backlog retain their published scope until deliberately synchronized; no board status, implementation progress or school approval is established by this documentation revision.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=t(r,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
